# REA Archive — working development version

This application creates, inspects, verifies and extracts bounded ZIP, 7Z, TAR, TAR.GZ, GZIP, Zstandard and TAR.ZST files. It is **not a complete replacement for 7-Zip 26.04**, and no full-product improvement has been demonstrated. All documented features of the original remain part of the requested acceptance scope.

## Run and verify

From `products/rea-studio`, run `npm start`, open the printed local address and select **Lab Archive**. To run application tests:

```text
node apps/archive/bootstrap-original.mjs
node --test --test-concurrency=1 apps/archive/app.test.mjs apps/archive/app.integration.test.mjs apps/archive/zip.test.mjs apps/archive/formats.test.mjs apps/archive/zstd.test.mjs apps/archive/sevenzip.test.mjs
node apps/archive/verify-sevenzip.mjs
```

Create a text fixture using the new-file form or put regular files inside the Archive application's dedicated workspace. ZIP, 7Z, TAR, TAR.GZ and TAR.ZST take a relative source folder; GZIP and Zstandard take one regular source file. Choose the format and a matching new output path: `.zip`, `.7z`, `.tar`, `.tar.gz`/`.tgz`, `.gz`, `.zst`, or `.tar.zst`/`.tzst`. ZIP supports **store**, **deflate** and **zstd**. 7Z supports **store**, **deflate**, **lzma** and **lzma2**, optionally combining files in one solid block and compressing the header. Its default is genuine LZMA2 with the own balanced profile. LZMA profiles use 64 KiB, 1 MiB or 8 MiB dictionaries and are not original 7-Zip level equivalents. ZIP/7Z DEFLATE and GZIP use compression levels 0–9; Zstandard uses levels 1–19, default 3. Inspection decompresses supported compressed data and checks the applicable format fields before reporting entries. Extraction requires a confirmation and a destination folder that does not already exist. The tested runtime is Node 24.16.0 with libzstd 1.5.7; its standard-library Zstandard API is marked experimental.

## Implemented behavior

- Genuine 7Z containers use Copy (`00`), raw DEFLATE (`040108`), LZMA (`030101`) or LZMA2 (`21`), with UTF-16LE names, empty files/directories, UTC FILETIME modification dates and optional solid substreams. Ordinary original default compressed data and LZMA encoded headers are accepted. One simple coder per folder and plain or one supported encoded header are accepted. Start/header CRCs and every recorded pack/folder/file CRC are verified; optional absent content CRCs are reported as unrecorded. Solid stored bytes are shown as shared rather than inventing per-file compressed sizes. The new greedy encoder can produce larger archives than original 7-Zip; LZMA2 resets history every 32 KiB and uses honest stored fallback for incompressible chunks. Independent comparisons, source assistance, bounds and memory copies are documented in [SEVENZIP-COMPATIBILITY.md](SEVENZIP-COMPATIBILITY.md) and [LZMA-COMPATIBILITY.md](LZMA-COMPATIBILITY.md).
- Single-disk classic ZIP and optional ZIP64 creation, including actual 64-bit extra fields, ZIP64 end record and locator. ZIP64 uses the same local size limits as classic ZIP. The reader resolves ZIP64 size, offset, disk and count sentinels and validates both signed and unsigned 32-bit/64-bit data descriptors, including streaming entries whose ZIP64 sizes appear only in the central directory. The filesystem creation form emits seekable headers; data-descriptor creation is available in the ZIP module API.
- ZIP creation writes UTF-8 names and optional archive comments. Reading supports UTF-8, legacy CP437 and recognized Info-ZIP Unicode Path extras whose filename CRC matches. ZIP entries use store, DEFLATE method 8 or Zstandard method 93 with version-needed 6.3. Reading also accepts method 93 in ZIP64 and signed/unsigned descriptor layouts. The withdrawn method 20 alias is rejected. Node's standard-library zlib supplies raw DEFLATE and Zstandard. Listing and extraction verify decompressed size and CRC-32, local/central header agreement, central-directory bounds and nonoverlapping entry ranges.
- Source ZIP modification, access and creation dates are stored in NTFS extras at millisecond precision. Reading prefers NTFS dates, then extended Unix modification time, then DOS local wall time. DOS dates have two-second precision and carry no timezone; extraction interprets them in the local timezone. Extraction restores modification/access times, with directory dates restored after their children. Creation timestamps are reported when present but are not restored to the filesystem.
- TAR creates ustar headers with PAX records for long/Unicode paths and timestamps that need them. Reading accepts regular files and directories, ustar prefixes, PAX local/global records and GNU long names. TAR validates header checksums and structure; the format has no payload CRC. File contents can change without invalidating a TAR header checksum.
- GZIP uses RFC 1952 headers, raw DEFLATE and verified payload CRC-32/size trailers. Reading accepts optional extra, filename, comment and header-CRC fields, and validates concatenated members. Standalone GZIP extraction joins members into one file and uses the first member's filename/date when present; otherwise its filename comes from the archive basename. GZIP filenames/comments use Latin-1. Creation omits a filename outside Latin-1 rather than misencoding it. TAR.GZ wraps the supported TAR container in GZIP.
- Zstandard uses genuine native compressed frames, emitted with a content checksum and a bounded 8 MiB compression history window. Reading explicitly walks every standard and skippable frame, joins concatenated frame contents and rejects trailing bytes. Every content checksum present is verified by the native decoder; the optional Zstandard checksum is the low 32 bits of XXH64, not CRC-32. Skippable metadata is ignored. A stream without content checksums has no payload checksum guarantee. Standalone `.zst` has no embedded filename or timestamp; extraction uses its archive basename and does not restore dates. TAR.ZST preserves the supported TAR paths and dates inside the compressed stream.
- Regular binary files, Unicode ZIP/TAR paths, empty files and empty directories survive round trips. Archives can be reopened after the host restarts.
- Extraction validates every entry before writing any file. Traversal, absolute paths, drive/alternate-stream paths, Windows reserved paths, symbolic-link entries, duplicate/case-colliding paths and file/directory path collisions are refused.
- New-file and archive creation refuse existing files. Extraction builds validated files in a new staging folder and exclusively claims a new destination before moving them into place. A failed final move may leave a partial new destination; existing destinations are never overwritten.

Limits are local implementation bounds, including when ZIP64 headers are used:

| Format | Entry/output limits | Container limits |
| --- | --- | --- |
| ZIP / ZIP64 | 10,000 entries; 16 MiB per file; 64 MiB total uncompressed bytes | 68 MiB ZIP input |
| 7Z | 10,000 entries/folders/pack streams/substreams; 16 MiB per file; 64 MiB total file and solid output bytes | 96 MiB archive; 16 MiB header; 100,000 metadata properties/chunks; 128 path components |
| TAR | 10,000 output entries; 16 MiB per file; 64 MiB total file bytes | 96 MiB TAR container; 64 KiB per metadata record |
| TAR.GZ | Same TAR file/entry limits | 96 MiB compressed input and 96 MiB inflated TAR container |
| Standalone GZIP | 16 MiB combined inflated output; up to 10,000 members | 96 MiB compressed input |
| Standalone Zstandard | 16 MiB combined decoded output | 96 MiB input; 64 MiB maximum decoder history window; 10,000 standard/skippable frames; 100,000 blocks |
| TAR.ZST | Same TAR file/entry limits; 96 MiB decoded TAR container | Same Zstandard input/window/frame/block limits |

Paths are limited to 4,096 characters by the shared path validator. GZIP filename/comment fields have a 4,096-character limit. Zstandard frames inside each ZIP entry use the same window/frame/block limits and that entry's declared decoded size; the existing 16 MiB file, 64 MiB total and 68 MiB ZIP bounds still apply. Unsupported methods, encryption and multi-disk layouts are rejected. Nonzero Zstandard dictionary IDs are rejected; no dictionary is supplied to the native codec. Dictionary-dependent frames without an ID fail native decoding. These formats are processed synchronously in memory; they do not provide streaming extraction of large archives or background compression workers.

7Z reading permits a declared LZMA/LZMA2 dictionary up to 64 MiB; writing permits 4 KiB through 8 MiB. LZMA2 writing uses chunks of 1–32 KiB and at most 65,536 chunks; reading permits up to 100,000 chunks. HTTP operations and direct 7Z container operations use a shared cooperative budget, default 15 seconds and 2 billion work units, across file collection, codecs, header processing, CRC checks and verification. Explicit API limits can be lowered or raised within 60 seconds and 8 billion units. Checks occur periodically and between bounded operations; native copies, zlib, filesystem waits and garbage collection cannot be interrupted by this mechanism. Memory keeps several bounded copies and is not constrained to the output size or a fixed RSS ceiling. See the exact accounting and limitations in the LZMA document.

The host confines paths to the Archive application's local workspace. The default example paths are `archive/source/hello.txt`, `archive/hello.zip` and `archive/restored`, all relative to that workspace. Inputs from another application's workspace are not automatically visible.

## Concrete parity gaps and limitations

Other 7Z coders/filters/complex bindings, external metadata, XZ, BZIP2, WIM and 7-Zip's many other read-only formats; multi-volume support; archive encryption; password handling; AES-256 7z/ZIP encryption; self-extracting archives; Windows Shell/FAR integration; the original file manager and command-line syntax; localization; archive update/delete/rename; complete timestamp, permission, ACL and extended-attribute preservation; streaming large archives; compression parallelism are absent. TAR links, sparse files, devices and other special entries are rejected. TAR ownership, mode/ACL and extended attributes are not restored. ZIP legacy names outside CP437 or a valid Unicode Path extra need an explicit encoding policy that is not implemented.

On Windows, ZIP checks use the independent .NET implementation in both directions and compare DOS-date extraction against a separately created/extracted .NET archive. Preserved original 7-Zip 26.04 also validates generated ZIP/ZIP64 files, frozen sentinel/streaming compatibility fixtures and exact extracted bytes; ZIP files generated by the original are replayed through this parser. The original binary is extracted from the verified release asset and checked by SHA-256 before use. Those Windows oracle checks are skipped on other platforms; the portable DOS regression runs in a fixed New York timezone. Tests establish the recorded round trips and malformed-input refusals, not exhaustive interoperability, adversarial review, optimum compression or faster execution than 7-Zip. The implementation imposes substantially smaller data limits than the original.

Zstandard tests authenticate the complete official 7-Zip 26.04 x64 installer and its extracted `7z.exe`/`7z.dll` before running them privately as independent decoding oracles. The verification-only bootstrap above authenticates existing local backups or downloads the exact official installer and `7zr.exe` from their pinned release URLs, checks byte lengths/SHA-256 and caches them under ignored `.local/archive-original`; vendor executable bytes are excluded from publication. Existing ZIP/TAR/GZIP original checks use the separately preserved original Extra acquisition. That original release decodes Zstandard and ZIP method 93 but does not encode them. Candidate-to-original checks therefore cover standalone Zstandard, concatenated/skippable frames, TAR.ZST and classic/ZIP64 method 93. The opposite direction uses digest-pinned Windows bsdtar 3.8.8/libzstd 1.5.7 to encode independent `.zst` and PAX TAR.ZST fixtures; both this application and original 7-Zip recover their exact bytes. A separately constructed ZIP 93 envelope wraps that independent compressed payload; it is the single synthetic ZIP fixture publication exception. HTTP checks cover create/list/test/extract, refusal before writes and reopening the same archives after host restart. These are qualified codec/container comparisons, not proof that 7-Zip can encode Zstandard or that this application supports all original features. The tested bytes and provenance are recorded in `zstd-validation.json`; fixture licensing and acquisition are in `fixtures/oracles/UPSTREAM-NOTICE.md`.

Official baseline reference reviewed on 2026-10-12: [7-Zip 26.04 and its feature list](https://www.7-zip.org/). ZIP layout was checked against [PKWARE APPNOTE 6.3.10](https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/casestudies/APPNOTE.TXT), particularly sections 4.3.9, 4.3.14–16, 4.4.6, 4.4.19–24, 4.5.3, 4.5.5 and 4.6.9. The downloaded reference and earlier implementation/test results are in unchanged historical receipts `compatibility-evidence.json`, `formats-validation.json`, `zstd-validation.json` and `sevenzip-validation.json`, bound to their frozen source snapshots. The prior 68-test 7Z source/receipt also remains in `results/lzma-stage/prior-app`. Current compressed LZMA/LZMA2 identities and checks are in `lzma-validation.json`; exact staged decoder/writer and failed ratio assertions are preserved under `results/lzma-stage`. The original acquisition records are under [`projects/08-7zip`](../../../../projects/08-7zip). These references are not a complete feature inventory for the original.

The Zstandard frame structure was checked against the retained [Zstandard format specification 0.4.3 from libzstd 1.5.7](https://github.com/facebook/zstd/blob/v1.5.7/doc/zstd_compression_format.md); the native API against the retained [Node 24.16.0 zlib documentation](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v24.16.0/docs/api/zlib.html). Reading walks each frame explicitly because the tested native one-shot decoder stops after the first frame. Exact source hashes are in the Zstandard receipt.

Application code contains original ZIP/TAR/GZIP/7Z containers, a Zstandard frame validator using Node standard-library zlib, and source-assisted LZMA/LZMA2 primitives. The container uses pinned official format notes and reader/writer study; the new primitives derive from public-domain SDK algorithms and pinned MIT decoder/encoder study with all permission notices retained in `LZMA-LICENSE.txt`. They import no original executable or DLL. Original vendor programs are used only as independent test oracles.
